{"type":"text","timestamp":1778369370172,"sessionID":"ses_1f0ed0c92ffexvWp3qykd5Jujr","part":{"id":"prt_e0f1317db001taANzXrbPLdhkR","messageID":"msg_e0f1303f9001cVsrgd4JRtMrZF","sessionID":"ses_1f0ed0c92ffexvWp3qykd5Jujr","type":"text","text":"The issue context is a single long JSON line and was truncated by the file reader, so I’m going to pretty-print it once to capture the full request before editing.","time":{"start":1778369370075,"end":1778369370171},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0736d609f89c15b80169ffc35a0e788191b72863b0aa4eb273","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778369370568,"sessionID":"ses_1f0ed0c92ffexvWp3qykd5Jujr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_cEdCFv8MAO9aaxPMTBFfpYx2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"jq . __issue_context__.json","timeout":120000,"workdir":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl","description":"Pretty-prints imported issue context"},"output":"{\n  \"body\": \"**CL term**\\n- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \\n- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \\n\\nThese classes are currently used with *in taxon: Homo sapiens*.\\n\\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\\nFor both parent classes (CL_0001204 and CL_0001203), please remove:\\n1. **CD44-high**  \\n2. **CD122-high**\\nRemoving these markers from the parent classes will correctly update all subclasses inheriting from these CD45RO-positive memory T cell terms.\\n\\n**Improved definitions**\\n**- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive **\\n\\nA CD4-positive, alpha-beta long-lived T cell with the phenotype CD45RO-positive and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\\n\\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224 \\n\\n\\n**- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  **\\n\\nA CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell with memory phenotype indicated by being CD45RO and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\\n\\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224 \\n\\n\\n\\n**Rationale**\\n**1. CD44-high is not a defining marker for human memory T cells**\\nIn human peripheral blood, CD44 is expressed at high levels on most CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells, including naïve, central memory, and effector memory populations.  \\nTherefore, CD44-high is not used to distinguish memory subsets in humans.CD44-based naïve vs memory separation is specific to mouse immunophenotyping.\\n\\n**2. CD122-high is characteristic of specific memory-like subsets, not all CD45RO-positive memory T cells**\\nExpression of IL-2Rβ (CD122) is variable across human memory T-cell subsets. High expression of CD122 is a feature of T stem cell memory (T_SCM) and certain innate/virtual memory–like subsets, but not of all CD45RO-positive memory T cells such as: central memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_CM)effector memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_EM)Thus, enforcing CD122-high at the parent level over constrains the phenotype of typical human memory T cells.\\n\\n**3. The current CD44-high and CD122-high constraints originate from mouse memory T cell biology**\\nCD44-high (and, in some contexts, CD122-high) is widely used in mouse memory-like CD8⁺ T-cell phenotyping. However, these markers do not define human memory T-cell subsets. Removing these axioms will make the human memory T-cell hierarchy more accurate and species-appropriate.\\n\\nFor these reasons, removing CD44-high and CD122-high from the parent classes CL_0001204 and CL_0001203 will make the human memory T cell hierarchy more accurate and biologically appropriate.\\n\\n**References**  \\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224  *Table 4 includes the markers used to identify memory T-cell subsets.\\n\",\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"[Class hierarchy] Remove CD44-high and CD122-high from CD45RO-positive memory T cells\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3454\"\n}\n","metadata":{"output":"{\n  \"body\": \"**CL term**\\n- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \\n- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \\n\\nThese classes are currently used with *in taxon: Homo sapiens*.\\n\\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\\nFor both parent classes (CL_0001204 and CL_0001203), please remove:\\n1. **CD44-high**  \\n2. **CD122-high**\\nRemoving these markers from the parent classes will correctly update all subclasses inheriting from these CD45RO-positive memory T cell terms.\\n\\n**Improved definitions**\\n**- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive **\\n\\nA CD4-positive, alpha-beta long-lived T cell with the phenotype CD45RO-positive and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\\n\\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224 \\n\\n\\n**- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  **\\n\\nA CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell with memory phenotype indicated by being CD45RO and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\\n\\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224 \\n\\n\\n\\n**Rationale**\\n**1. CD44-high is not a defining marker for human memory T cells**\\nIn human peripheral blood, CD44 is expressed at high levels on most CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells, including naïve, central memory, and effector memory populations.  \\nTherefore, CD44-high is not used to distinguish memory subsets in humans.CD44-based naïve vs memory separation is specific to mouse immunophenotyping.\\n\\n**2. CD122-high is characteristic of specific memory-like subsets, not all CD45RO-positive memory T cells**\\nExpression of IL-2Rβ (CD122) is variable across human memory T-cell subsets. High expression of CD122 is a feature of T stem cell memory (T_SCM) and certain innate/virtual memory–like subsets, but not of all CD45RO-positive memory T cells such as: central memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_CM)effector memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_EM)Thus, enforcing CD122-high at the parent level over constrains the phenotype of typical human memory T cells.\\n\\n**3. The current CD44-high and CD122-high constraints originate from mouse memory T cell biology**\\nCD44-high (and, in some contexts, CD122-high) is widely used in mouse memory-like CD8⁺ T-cell phenotyping. However, these markers do not define human memory T-cell subsets. Removing these axioms will make the human memory T-cell hierarchy more accurate and species-appropriate.\\n\\nFor these reasons, removing CD44-high and CD122-high from the parent classes CL_0001204 and CL_0001203 will make the human memory T cell hierarchy more accurate and biologically appropriate.\\n\\n**References**  \\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224  *Table 4 includes the markers used to identify memory T-cell subsets.\\n\",\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"[Class hierarchy] Remove CD44-high and CD122-high from CD45RO-positive memory T cells\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3454\"\n}\n","exit":0,"description":"Pretty-prints imported issue context","truncated":false},"title":"Pretty-prints imported issue context","time":{"start":1778369370561,"end":1778369370566}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0736d609f89c15b80169ffc35a258c8191b24f564fbfef8c07"}},"id":"prt_e0f13183c001Kla2rRGrMnVJPL","sessionID":"ses_1f0ed0c92ffexvWp3qykd5Jujr","messageID":"msg_e0f1303f9001cVsrgd4JRtMrZF"}}
